# Pre-Commit Scrutiny

This is a public repository. Commit history is permanent and visible to everyone. Every commit must be clean before it lands.

## When This Rule Applies

Before any git commit, staged changes, or push operation, the agent MUST scan all staged files for prohibited patterns. No exceptions — even documentation-only changes must be scanned.

## Prohibited Patterns

Halt the commit immediately if any of the following are found:

| Pattern | Why It Must Not Appear |
|---|---|
| `xcaffold-platform` | Internal project name |
| `assembly.floor`, `cloud.hub` | Proprietary internal terms |
| `/Volumes/Projects/` | Machine-specific absolute path |
| `/Users/<any-username>/` | Machine-specific absolute path |
| `Phase N`, `sprint`, `roadmap`, `milestone` | Internal process terminology |
| `premium`, `enterprise`, `saas` | Proprietary product tier names |

Also check commit messages and code comments for the following — these terms are acceptable in documentation and comparison contexts but MUST NOT appear in commit messages or inline code comments referencing internal workflows:

- Third-party AI tool names used in a context that reveals internal workflow details

## Scan Command

Run this before staging any commit:

```bash
git diff --cached -- . | grep -inE '(xcaffold-platform|assembly\.floor|cloud\.hub|/Volumes/Projects/|/Users/[^/]+/|Phase [0-9]|sprint|roadmap|milestone|premium|enterprise|saas)' || echo "clean"
```

If the output is anything other than `clean`, stop immediately.

## Recovery Procedure

1. Halt the commit process
2. Identify every file containing a prohibited pattern
3. Replace or remove the prohibited content with neutral, feature-oriented language
4. Re-run the scan command to confirm clean output
5. Proceed with the commit only after the scan returns `clean`

## No Exceptions

Minor changes and documentation updates are not exempt. A single prohibited term in a `// comment` or a markdown file is enough to block the commit.
